364594	4349	0	Now that I've got you nice and worried, I want to take a quick second to chat about risk. For those that have never travelled, it might seem strange to hop in the van of a stranger after two minutes of conversation. You'll see, however, that an important common theme in all these stories is that surrendering to the kindness of strangers is a calculated risk. Sometimes things can go wrong, but rarely catastrophically. Things can be awkward, for sure, as you will see. They can be physically uncomfortable. They can be unpredictable. But rarely do they end up life threatening.\n\nI say this because I know you, dear reader, might have leapt, perhaps unconsciously, to some light judgement. \n\n"How could you take such a risk? You had no idea what would happen! You didn't know this guy at all"\nI agree. {{second quote}} It could have ended in a massive fight. I might have had to hitch a lift back to the town. I might have needed to call the police. \n\nTwo things help me with this kind of peace in chaos. Firstly, you develop a kind of radar for bad (and good) intentions. It's by no means perfect, but after years of travelling and meeting hundreds of people on the road, you have a strong gut feeling for people that just want to help. Secondly, and maybe more importantly, I set the bar somewhere else. For me, a day where I don't eat can be a good day and a good story. Where I'm harassed by police. Where I'm scammed. As long as I come out alive, healthy and not too traumatised, I'm happy. Most people, quite reasonably, draw the line at discomfort. They would, quite reasonably, make different decisions. And so I say it's worth keeping one question in mind when hearing travel stories: what's really at stake? It may be that you're setting the bar somewhere very different from the daring raconteur in front of you. This day, it seems the bar was set above riding 100km {{check}} with a fever. \n\n
364736	4930	0	{{TODO: Trim this aside and \n
